{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review the resident-rebuild progress at exact clean head ca7d692dd51889c2b942e7e8794fef19a0d6fcf6, artifact 01d4f0979f36bbd8aba33a3b60d84a5164855565.\n\nThis is the work you asked for after workroom-fold@2 left the browser empty for minutes, and it is a consequence of my own change, so I would rather you were sceptical than generous.\n\nThe design question I raised before building it is now built the way I proposed, and you did not get a chance to object first. Progress publishes into atomics inside scanHead's verification loop; Reader.AuditProgress reads them without r.mu and Workspace.RebuildProgress without snapshotMu. That touches the kernel. The argument for it being safe is that the counter is write-only from the verifier's side, no control flow reads it, and deleting it would change no decision about what is accepted — but the argument is mine, and the alternative I named at the time was coarser and left the kernel alone. If you prefer that, say so and I will rebuild it that way rather than defend this one.\n\nWhat to attack hardest, in order.\n\nThe lock-free claim. Both accessors are useless if anything they touch can be held. I tested it by polling from another goroutine while a real audit runs, and deliberately never joined the poller — joining turns a wrong design into a hung test rather than a failing one, which is exactly what my first draft did, timing out at ten minutes.\n\nThe endpoint. /v0/rebuild must answer while Snapshot is blocked, or nothing above it matters. The test holds snapshotMu directly rather than simulating a slow audit.\n\nSomething I could not close: my mutation check on that endpoint — routing it back through Snapshot — hung past sixty seconds instead of failing at the test's own ten-second deadline. It compiled, so it was not a build error. I did not diagnose it. The positive property is directly tested and passes, but I am telling you the negative is unproven rather than implying otherwise, and I suspect the same join-shaped mistake.\n\nThe condition I met by refusing it. You allowed a previously verified projection to remain readable under a rebuilding qualifier. After a profile bump there is no such projection — the checkpoint is invalid under a contract the binary no longer implements — so anything shown would be unverified here. Progress with no work data is what you already permit for first startup. Stale rows behind a reassuring label would satisfy the letter and invert the point. That is a judgement about your request, not an implementation detail, and it is the thing most worth disagreeing with.\n\nThe profile mismatch is reproduced end to end over scaled history, asserting loaded.Full so it cannot pass vacuously on a warm path, then that progress advances, then that the verified projection for the exact head and depth is what replaces it.\n\nGates: go vet clean; go test ./internal/... ./cmd/... exit 0, 15 packages; race clean on the progress test; tsc clean; UI 73/73; make ui-check green with the embed regenerated; clean worktree; current main 8f22ba52 an ancestor.\n\nDo not merge.","body":{"branch":"request/resident-rebuild-progress","conditions":"A review verdict of approved or changes-requested is reported against exact head ca7d692dd51889c2b942e7e8794fef19a0d6fcf6, judging artifact 01d4f0979f36bbd8aba33a3b60d84a5164855565 cited directly: that progress is genuinely readable while both the reader and snapshot mutexes are held, and that the kernel change cannot affect what the verifier accepts; that /v0/rebuild answers while a snapshot is in flight and serves no projection; that the browser reports a measured phase without implying failure or currentness and stays silent when warm; that the profile-mismatch test exercises the cold path rather than passing vacuously; and a judgement on whether refusing to serve a stale projection under a rebuilding qualifier is the right reading of that condition or a shortfall.","head":"ca7d692dd51889c2b942e7e8794fef19a0d6fcf6","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}